## Lessons are what the gates taught

```
/lessons:new <finding>  record what a bounced gate taught, as one imperative line
/lessons:retro          merge duplicates, retire what no longer bites, stay inside the budget
/lessons:lint           validate the layer and the tracker's links into it

python3 .claude/scripts/lessons.py for <agent>   # the rules that bind it, capped and ranked
python3 .claude/scripts/lessons.py show <LESSON-NNNN>
```

Each rule is one line of at most 120 characters, and the hand-off is capped at
`policy.lesson_budget` of them so that reading them stays cheaper than
rediscovering them. A ticket that **bounced** — `review -> in_progress`,
`in_progress -> writing_tests`, `writing_tests -> speccing`, `speccing -> todo`
— owes one, filed by whoever took that edge at the moment they took it.
Confirming an existing rule (`lessons.py confirm <ID> --ticket <ID>`) counts,
and beats a near-duplicate. The specification is
[`docs/lessons/README.md`](docs/lessons/README.md); the procedure is
`.claude/skills/agile-lessons/SKILL.md`.

Agents: {{AGENT_LIST}} in `.claude/agents/`. Their operating rules live in
`.claude/skills/agile-{artifacts,grooming,git,dod,lessons}/` and
`.claude/skills/project-knowledge/`.

## Rules that are not negotiable

- **Every `INDEX.md` is generated.** Never edit `docs/agile/INDEX.md`,
  `docs/knowledge/INDEX.md` or `docs/lessons/INDEX.md`. Change the ticket, the
  note or the lesson and let `agile.py index` / `kb.py index` /
  `lessons.py index` regenerate them. A hook denies the write anyway.
- **Every task and bug carries `spec:` and `docs:`.** A `PreToolUse` hook
  refuses a new ticket missing either, because those two fields are what make
  the grilling step and the consult step happen at all. `spec:` is
  `specs/<ID>.md` or null with `spec_waiver: NO-SPEC (<reason>)`.
- **Every task and bug carries `docs:`.** It is the knowledge-base notes read at
  grooming and actualised at close. A ticket at `review` or beyond with an empty
  `docs:` and no `docs_waiver: NO-DOCS (<reason>)` fails `agile.py lint` — the
  same idiom as `NO-TEST` and `SKIPPED`: not everything must be documented, but
  a decision not to document must be visible and argued.
- **A note is created by `kb.py new`, never by hand.** The path, the frontmatter
  and the required sections all come out right; a `PreToolUse` hook refuses a
  misfiled or misnamed file under `docs/knowledge/`. `overview` is not the
  default type — a rule is a `business-rule`, an incident is `troubleshooting`,
  an external system is an `integration`.
- **A ticket says what it taught before it closes.** At `verify` and `done`,
  `lessons:` names the rule this ticket produced or confirmed, or
  `lessons_waiver: NO-LESSON (<reason>)` says it taught nothing — the same idiom
  as `NO-DOCS`. The waiver is the normal answer for a ticket that ran straight
  through, and a rejection for one that bounced: that bounce is the only
  evidence this harness produces that the *process* failed rather than the code.
- **A task's `scope` is exactly one** of {{SCOPE_LIST}}. Anything spanning two
  is two tasks under one story.
- **One active ticket per working tree**, counting `writing_tests` as active. A
  second concurrent ticket in the same repository corrupts the first.
- **The cycle is specification-first, then test-first.** `qa` claims a ticket
  out of `todo` into `speccing`, grills the request into a numbered brief at
  `docs/agile/specs/<ID>.md`, then writes a failing test per requirement and
  proves it red *before* an engineer starts. There is no `todo -> writing_tests`
  and no `todo -> in_progress`. Where nothing can be grilled, `qa` records
  `spec_waiver: NO-SPEC (<reason>)`; where no automated test is possible, a
  `NO-TEST (<reason>)` waiver naming the substitute check.
- **QA does not guess, and does not ask.** At the spec gate it answers what the
  knowledge base and the code can answer, and everything left becomes a batch of
  questions that **the top-level session** puts to the user, in one go, in the
  user's language. An unanswered question becomes carryover — a recorded
  decision — never an assumption. A requirement `qa` had to settle alone is
  marked `assumed` and is argued again at validation.
- **`speccing` does not occupy a working tree.** It cuts no branch and writes
  only the tracker, so the next ticket can be specified while this one is being
  implemented. Everything from `writing_tests` onward does occupy it, and the
  one-ticket-per-tree rule counts from there.
- **Dispatch prompts are built, not written.**
  `python3 .claude/scripts/agile.py handoff <ID> --gate <N>` produces them:
  binding instructions first, repeated last, with reference material in between
  as paths and commands rather than pasted files. A rule buried in the middle of
  a long prompt is a rule that will not be followed — do not move the anchor, do
  not splice a preamble in front of the contract, and do not paste file bodies
  the agent can read for itself.
- **A requirement that is not in the brief is not built.** If the work turns out
  to need one, the engineer says so and hands back; it was never grilled, never
  costed and never agreed. Deferred requirements are not dropped either: `pm`
  files a ticket per carryover row at the last gate, or argues
  `NO-TICKET (<reason>)` in the open.
- **An engineer never edits the tests `qa` wrote.** It continues on `qa`'s
  branch and implements until they pass. A test it believes is wrong goes back
  via `in_progress -> writing_tests`, argued in `## Log` — never edited, skipped
  or loosened. QA diffs the test files at the review gate, and that diff is
  expected to be empty.
- **Nobody closes their own work.** An engineer stops at `review`, QA moves it to
  `verify`, `verify` means awaiting the user's acceptance, and only the top-level
  session sets `done` inside `/agile:close`.
- **An unavailable check is `SKIPPED (<reason> — see TASK-000X)`, never a pass.**
  The matrix is `python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py checks <scope>`.
- **Every code commit carries a `Refs: <TICKET-ID>` trailer.** Conventional
  Commits, third-person verb: `feat: adds price per day to the subscription
  resource`.
- Never commit anything on the project's never-commit list (`git.never_commit`
  in `forge.json`). Never push with `--no-verify`.

## Verify your work before handing it back

```bash
python3 .claude/scripts/agile.py lint     # exit 2 means the tracker is inconsistent
python3 .claude/scripts/kb.py lint        # exit 2 means the knowledge base is inconsistent
python3 .claude/scripts/lessons.py lint   # exit 2 means a rule drifted, duplicated or went stale
python3 .claude/scripts/forge.py doctor   # exit 2 means the harness config drifted
```
